As a short sword, it's -1.5 damage against small creaturs and -2.5 vs.
large creatures.  That's nerfing it for non-Samurai, and I'm not sure
that S really need _more_ combat skill.

I'm not sure how it's finally "a good weapon" for tourists or most
healers, either.

Those classes are both start as neutral, so are unlikely to get it.
If they do, even taking weapon skill into account it does the same
damage for tourists (+.5 vs small monsters, -.5 vs large monsters) as
currently.  For healers who sac'd for it, it does less damage than
currently (-.5/-1.5) once skill is maxed out (it's worse in both cases
until skill is maxed).

If you're wishing for something, you'd much rather get Grayswandir or
another better artifact anyway, so in practice the only people it does
more damage for as a short sword are healers who find it randomly or
in a bones pile.

Not a common case, and even then it's only marginally better than a
unihorn and still obsolete by the Quest.

(Much less importantly, by eliminating a long sword artifact, you make
Excalibur a bit worse for lawfuls who are restricted in long sword.)



